    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Beginning in 230 BCE, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-shi-huang">Ying Zheng</a> of Qin conquered Han, Zhao, Yan, Wei, Chu and Qi in turn, and in 221 BCE took the new title Shi Huangdi, 'First Emperor'.</li><li>The empire was divided into 36 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">commanderies</a> and more than 1,000 districts run by appointed officials, ending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristocracy">aristocratic rule</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> inherited this administrative state.</li><li>The dynasty's 14 years make it the shortest of any major Chinese dynasty, with only two emperors, yet it is credited with originating the imperial system that lasted until 1911.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Qin is the likeliest origin of the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>: the term appears in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> as Cina and Sina, then in Greek as Thinai or Sinai, before entering the languages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

