      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Rise &amp; Fall</h2>
      <figure class="rf-chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 760 206" role="img" aria-label="Relative power of the Qin dynasty over time, peaking in 214 BCE"><line class="rf-base" x1="20" x2="740" y1="160" y2="160"/><path class="rf-area" d="M20.0 39.4 L308.0 26.0 L356.0 26.0 L404.0 39.4 L548.0 52.8 L596.0 93.0 L692.0 133.2 L740.0 146.6 L740.0 160 L20.0 160 Z"/><path class="rf-line" d="M20.0 39.4 L308.0 26.0 L356.0 26.0 L404.0 39.4 L548.0 52.8 L596.0 93.0 L692.0 133.2 L740.0 146.6"/><circle class="rf-peak" cx="356.0" cy="26.0" r="5"/><text class="rf-peak-label" x="356.0" y="16.0" text-anchor="middle">Peak 214 BCE</text><g class="rf-ev"><line x1="20.0" x2="20.0" y1="160" y2="166"/><circle cx="20.0" cy="160" r="8"/><text x="20.0" y="164" text-anchor="middle">2</text><title>221 BCE: Qin conquers Qi, taking its capital at Linzi, and King Zheng declares himself Shi Huangdi, the First Emperor.</title></g><g class="rf-ev"><line x1="308.0" x2="308.0" y1="160" y2="166"/><circle cx="308.0" cy="160" r="8"/><text x="308.0" y="164" text-anchor="middle">3</text><title>215 BCE: The general Meng Tian conquers the Ordos region from the Xiongnu and agriculture is established there.</title></g><g class="rf-ev"><line x1="356.0" x2="356.0" y1="160" y2="166"/><circle cx="356.0" cy="160" r="8"/><text x="356.0" y="164" text-anchor="middle">4</text><title>214 BCE: Some 500,000 men are sent south against the Baiyue peoples; after heavy losses, Qin takes the coastal lands around Guangzhou and the provinces of Fuzhou and Guilin.</title></g><g class="rf-ev"><line x1="548.0" x2="548.0" y1="160" y2="166"/><circle cx="548.0" cy="160" r="8"/><text x="548.0" y="164" text-anchor="middle">5</text><title>210 BCE: Qin Shi Huang dies on a journey to the eastern frontiers; Li Si and Zhao Gao hide his death, alter his will and place Huhai on the throne as Qin Er Shi.</title></g><g class="rf-ev"><line x1="596.0" x2="596.0" y1="160" y2="166"/><circle cx="596.0" cy="160" r="8"/><text x="596.0" y="164" text-anchor="middle">6</text><title>209 BCE: Popular revolt breaks out as men across China attack officials, raise armies and declare themselves kings.</title></g><g class="rf-ev"><line x1="692.0" x2="692.0" y1="160" y2="166"/><circle cx="692.0" cy="160" r="8"/><text x="692.0" y="164" text-anchor="middle">7</text><title>207 BCE: Ziying, a son of Fusu, surrenders to Liu Bang on his arrival at Xianyang and is soon executed by Xiang Yu.</title></g><g class="rf-ev"><line x1="740.0" x2="740.0" y1="160" y2="166"/><circle cx="740.0" cy="160" r="8"/><text x="740.0" y="164" text-anchor="middle">8</text><title>206 BCE: Xianyang is destroyed, marking the end of the imperial Qin dynasty; the Chu–Han Contention follows.</title></g><text class="rf-axis" x="20" y="200">221 BCE</text><text class="rf-axis" x="740" y="200" text-anchor="end">206 BCE</text></svg><figcaption>Relative power on the Institute's 0–10 scale (each profile scaled to its own peak). Numbers mark the key events below.</figcaption></figure><ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">230 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Qin attacks the state of Han directly to its east and takes its capital city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinzheng">Xinzheng</a>, opening the wars of unification.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">221 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Qin conquers Qi, taking its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linzi">Linzi</a>, and King Zheng declares himself Shi Huangdi, the First Emperor.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">215 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meng-tian">Meng Tian</a> conquers the Ordos region from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> and agriculture is established there.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">214 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Some 500,000 men are sent south against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baiyue">Baiyue</a> peoples; after heavy losses, Qin takes the coastal lands around Guangzhou and the provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuzhou">Fuzhou</a> and Guilin.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">210 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-shi-huang">Qin Shi Huang</a> dies on a journey to the eastern frontiers; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-si">Li Si</a> and Zhao Gao hide his death, alter his will and place <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-er-shi">Huhai</a> on the throne as Qin Er Shi.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">209 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Popular revolt breaks out as men across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> attack officials, raise armies and declare themselves kings.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">207 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziying">Ziying</a>, a son of Fusu, surrenders to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a> on his arrival at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianyang">Xianyang</a> and is soon executed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiang-yu">Xiang Yu</a>.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">206 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianyang">Xianyang</a> is destroyed, marking the end of the imperial Qin dynasty; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chu-han-contention">Chu–Han Contention</a> follows.</span></li></ol>
